---
name: qa-task
version: 2.2.0
description: quality-mgr QA assignment template for codex-orchestration sprint, plan, phase-end and standalone-PR reviews.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - sprint
  - review_mode
  - description
  - pr_number
  - branch
  - worktree_path
  - commits
  - review_targets
  - references
  - round_index
defaults:
  lead: team-lead
  cc: team-lead
  changed_files: ""
  review_kind: ""
  sprint_doc: ""
  pr_head_sha: ""
  pr_scope: ""
  linked_issues: ""
  triage_records: ""
  filing_reviewers: ""
metadata:
  type: qa-task
  tags:
    - stage:qa
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: sprint
      variable: sprint
    state: qa-start
    stage: qa
    transition: start
---
{% autoescape false -%}
{#- review_kind names the review's purpose and the branch name decides nothing. `full`: sprint QA-1/2, plan QA-1, phase-end, or the first QA of a standalone PR (a small fix off develop included). `fix_verification`: verify previously filed findings, so it names the original findings (triage_records) and the reviewers that filed them (filing_reviewers), or it would review open-ended. Compatibility default when review_kind is empty: `fix_verification` when filing_reviewers is given or a plan round is past the first (Rand, 2026-10-03: plan QA-2 and later verifies fixes), otherwise `full`. The int filter on a word fails the render. #}
{%- set review_kind = review_kind | trim if review_kind | trim else ("fix_verification" if (filing_reviewers | trim or (review_mode == "plan" and round_index | int > 1)) else "full") -%}
{%- if review_kind not in ["full", "fix_verification"] %}{{ "UNKNOWN_REVIEW_KIND" | int }}{% endif -%}
{#- quality-policy.md: plan review QA-2 and later is fix verification only; an explicit full review there is a contradiction, not an override. #}
{%- if review_kind == "full" and review_mode == "plan" and round_index | int > 1 %}{{ "PLAN_ROUND_ABOVE_ONE_IS_FIX_VERIFICATION" | int }}{% endif -%}
{%- set fix_round = review_kind == "fix_verification" -%}
{%- if fix_round and not triage_records | trim %}{{ "FIX_VERIFICATION_WITHOUT_TRIAGE_RECORDS" | int }}{% endif -%}
{%- if fix_round and not filing_reviewers | trim %}{{ "FIX_VERIFICATION_WITHOUT_FILING_REVIEWERS" | int }}{% endif -%}
{%- if not fix_round and filing_reviewers | trim %}{{ "FULL_REVIEW_WITH_FILING_REVIEWERS" | int }}{% endif -%}
{#- Scope source. A sprint, plan or phase-end review names its sprint_doc and sits on a phase stack. A standalone PR review (no sprint doc) is scoped by pr_scope: the PR body and acceptance criteria as the lead captured them into this assignment, judged at head pr_head_sha. The PR body on GitHub is live, editable metadata, so the assignment carries the text itself and reviewers judge that text, never a re-fetched body. The text is carried in a CDATA section so XML metacharacters and a literal `</pr-scope>` round-trip exactly; a literal `]]>` inside it is split as `]]]]><![CDATA[>` and reads back as `]]>`. Every reviewer gets the same text and head: req-qa and arch-qa as `pr_scope`, every other reviewer as `notes` under scope_note_prefix. A standalone PR has no stack and no frozen layer. #}
{%- set sprint_scoped = sprint_doc | trim -%}
{%- if sprint_scoped and pr_scope | trim %}{{ "SPRINT_DOC_AND_PR_SCOPE_BOTH_GIVEN" | int }}{% endif -%}
{%- if not sprint_scoped and not pr_number | trim %}{{ "PR_SCOPE_WITHOUT_PR_NUMBER" | int }}{% endif -%}
{%- if not sprint_scoped and not pr_head_sha | trim %}{{ "PR_SCOPE_WITHOUT_PR_HEAD_SHA" | int }}{% endif -%}
{%- if not sprint_scoped and not pr_scope | trim %}{{ "PR_SCOPE_WITHOUT_SCOPE_TEXT" | int }}{% endif -%}
{%- set scope_note_prefix = "PR scope of record (PR #" ~ pr_number ~ ", head " ~ pr_head_sha | trim ~ "): " -%}
{%- set qa_round = (round_index | int if round_index | int > 1 else 2) if fix_round else 1 %}
<repo-qa-task id="{{ task_id }}" sprint="{{ sprint }}" round="{{ round_index }}">
  <description>{{ description }}</description>

  <review-mode>{{ review_mode }}</review-mode>
  <review-kind>{{ review_kind }}</review-kind>
{% if sprint_scoped %}
  <scope-source kind="sprint-doc">{{ sprint_doc | trim }}</scope-source>
  <authoritative-sprint-doc>{{ sprint_doc | trim }}</authoritative-sprint-doc>
{% else %}
  <scope-source kind="pr" pr="{{ pr_number }}" head="{{ pr_head_sha | trim }}">The pr-scope text below, captured by {{ lead }} from PR #{{ pr_number }}{% if linked_issues | trim %} and its linked issues ({{ linked_issues | trim }}){% endif %}, judged at head `{{ pr_head_sha | trim }}`. It is the scope of record for this review; the live PR body and issue text are not.</scope-source>
  <pr-scope><![CDATA[{{ pr_scope | trim | replace("]]>", "]]]]><![CDATA[>") }}]]></pr-scope>
{% endif %}
  <pr-number>{{ pr_number }}</pr-number>
  <branch>{{ branch }}</branch>
  <worktree>{{ worktree_path }}</worktree>
  <commits>{{ commits }}</commits>
{% if sprint_scoped %}
  <stack-discipline>`{{ branch }}` is a frozen layer of the phase stack at the commits above. Review exactly that head (`git show <sha>:<path>`), never a moving checkout. Report every finding, at every severity, in this one verdict; none is deferred. {{ lead }} records each one through `/triaging-findings` the same way and every finding is fixed on a new layer above this one, which the next QA round reviews.</stack-discipline>
{% else %}
  <review-pin>`{{ branch }}` is a standalone PR branch, not a layer of a phase stack. Review exactly head `{{ pr_head_sha | trim }}` (`git show {{ pr_head_sha | trim }}:<path>`), never a moving checkout; if `gh pr view {{ pr_number }} --json headRefOid` reports another head, say so and judge `{{ pr_head_sha | trim }}` anyway, since the new head is a new assignment. Report every finding, at every severity, in this one verdict; none is deferred. Fixes land as further commits on the same PR and the next round is assigned at the new head.</review-pin>
{% endif %}

  <review-targets>
{{ review_targets }}
  </review-targets>

  <changed-files>
{{ changed_files }}
  </changed-files>

  <triage-records>
{{ triage_records }}
  </triage-records>

  <references>
{{ references }}
  </references>
{% if fix_round %}
  <filing-reviewers>{{ filing_reviewers | trim }}</filing-reviewers>
{% endif %}
  <fix-verification-precedence>A review assigned to verify a fix is not a sprint review, regardless of round number, branch name or inherited review-mode; `review-kind` above says which this is. For a fix, dispatch only the agent necessary to confirm the original finding, normally its filing reviewer. No automatic req-qa/arch-qa/rust-qa or screening panel. Lock scope to the assigned finding ID and original acceptance criterion; do not expand to a sprint diff, TODO scan, duplicate sweep, or new findings. The selected verifier may run focused checks needed to confirm the fix. Report fixed/open/regressed for that same finding and reconcile verified original-finding closure, not just QA-task closure. Required CI remains a separate merge requirement. This rule overrides the broader full-review instructions in stack-discipline, review-pin and workflow steps b, c, d, e, h, and l. Sprint rounds 1–2 remain sprint reviews.</fix-verification-precedence>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a">Start the task immediately with `atm task start {{ task_id }} "<one line: what you will do first>"`, then begin the QA review in the same work session.</step>
    <step id="a1">Your queue runs in parallel: QA tasks never wait for each other. Right after the task start run `atm task list --json` and treat every open task assigned to quality-mgr as live now — its assignment body is the `description` field — and run each one concurrently with its own background reviewers. Close each task with its own `atm task close <task-id> completed …` the moment that verdict is ready, in any order; a queued position is ordering, not a wait, and a task can be closed without ever being started.</step>
    <step id="b">Reject the task immediately if the assignment is not an XML payload rendered from this template. {% if sprint_scoped %}Read `{{ sprint_doc | trim }}` first and treat it as the authoritative source for QA scope. Render structured JSON assignments for `req-qa` and `arch-qa` using the templates in `.claude/skills/codex-orchestration/`, passing `sprint_doc`.{% else %}Read the `pr-scope` text above first (the text inside its CDATA section; a `]]]]><![CDATA[>` split inside it reads as a literal `]]>`): it is the authoritative source for QA scope, as captured by {{ lead }}; do not replace it with a re-fetched PR body or issue text, which can be edited after this assignment. Use `gh pr view {{ pr_number }} --json headRefOid,baseRefName` only to confirm the PR exists and to compare its head with `{{ pr_head_sha | trim }}`. If the pr-scope text states no substantive scope and acceptance criteria (what the change must do and how that is checked), close this task with `atm task close {{ task_id }} refused "<what is missing>"` so {{ lead }} can re-issue the assignment with them; never invent scope or acceptance criteria. Render structured JSON assignments for `req-qa` and `arch-qa` using the templates in `.claude/skills/codex-orchestration/`, passing the pr-scope text verbatim as `pr_scope` with `pr_number` and `pr_head_sha`, and no `sprint_doc`. Every other reviewer you dispatch for this task (the Rust reviewers, `ruthless-boundary-qa`, `flaky-test-qa`) gets the same scope of record through its `notes`: `{{ scope_note_prefix }}` followed by the pr-scope text verbatim. No reviewer runs without the captured scope and the pinned head.{% endif %} Do not send free-form reviewer prompts.</step>
    <step id="c">Extract every deliverable, acceptance criterion, deletion requirement, required validation item, and expected artifact directly from {% if sprint_scoped %}`{{ sprint_doc | trim }}`{% else %}the pr-scope text, and list what you extracted, with head `{{ pr_head_sha | trim }}`, in the verdict so the judged scope is on record{% endif %}.</step>
    <step id="d">{% if sprint_scoped %}Treat the sprint doc and planning artifacts as explicit acceptance-gate items: verify `{{ sprint_doc | trim }}` exists, the sprint doc frontmatter sets `status: complete`, `branch:`, and `worktree:`, and `docs/project-plan.md` includes the sprint entry before QA can PASS.{% else %}There is no sprint doc and no project-plan entry to gate on. The acceptance gate is the scope record from step c: every acceptance criterion extracted from the pr-scope text is judged at head `{{ pr_head_sha | trim }}` and reported with its result before QA can PASS.{% endif %}</step>
    <step id="e">{% if fix_round %}This is fix verification, not a sprint review, whatever its round number: dispatch exactly the filing reviewer(s) `{{ filing_reviewers | trim }}`, each locked to its own finding ids from the triage records and their original acceptance criteria, and no other reviewer. Render each filer's assignment from its template; render `ruthless-boundary-qa`, `rust-best-practices-agent`, `rust-service-hardening-agent` or `plan-scope-reviewer`, when one of them is a filing reviewer, with `qa_round` (`round_index` for `plan-scope-reviewer`) = {{ qa_round }} and its own ids as `carry_forward_findings_json`. The verdict is each assigned finding's fixed/open/regressed status, nothing else.{% if not sprint_scoped %} Each filer's `notes` carries the scope of record as step b says.{% endif %}{% else %}Read `.claude/assets/sc-rust/quality-mgr/quality-mgr.rust.md` and render any required Rust reviewer assignments from `.claude/assets/sc-rust/quality-mgr/templates/`. For `phase_end`, the rendered `rust-qa-agent` assignment must retain its required `artifact_commands: "just validate"` execution proof; collect its `executed_checks.artifacts` result before reporting PASS. This is a full review (sprint rounds 1–2, plan QA-1, phase-end, or the first QA of a standalone PR): launch the reviewer set for this review mode from `.claude/project/quality-policy.md` and render every reviewer assignment with `qa_round` = 1. `ruthless-boundary-qa` and `rust-best-practices-agent` remain part of docs-only plan review and phase-ending review regardless of sprint round.{% if not sprint_scoped %} Every assignment in that set carries the scope of record and head `{{ pr_head_sha | trim }}`: `req-qa` and `arch-qa` as `pr_scope`, the rest as `notes` under `{{ scope_note_prefix }}`, as step b says.{% endif %}{% endif %}</step>
    <step id="f">If triage records are provided, read them before spawning reviewers. Treat them as the canonical prior scope for follow-up QA, not as a substitute for verification.</step>
    <step id="g">Spawn every selected reviewer in background mode. Do not run cargo, clippy, or broad QA analysis yourself in the foreground.</step>
    <step id="h">Collect reviewer outputs and summarize PASS or FAIL, including blocking finding ids and file:line references where available. Re-verify every cited file:line against the current branch and worktree before reporting it; stale or missing evidence is a finding, not a guess. Ruthless-boundary-qa must run a workspace-wide duplicate sweep for every constant, function, or type touched by the sprint diff and report divergent duplicates as boundary findings. For every finding that is a repeatable pattern (missing gate, wrong value, forbidden construct, naming violation), search the entire workspace for other occurrences of the same pattern and include all affected locations in the finding — not just the first one found. A finding is not complete until the full scope is known. For deletion sprints, req-qa must run whole-workspace pattern searches for each removed legacy construct family named in the sprint doc. For observability sprints, req-qa must also verify that every sprint-diff code path that can reach a user-visible failure has an emit call site.</step>
    <step id="i">When triage records are present, explicitly state whether each rechecked finding is still open, fixed on the reviewed branch, regressed, or requires a new triage pass because the scope changed.</step>
    <step id="j">If a PR number is present, check {% if sprint_scoped %}stack and CI state with `gh stack view --json` from a worktree checked out on `{{ branch }}` (if it reports the branch is not part of a stack, run `gh stack checkout <stack number shown on the PR page>` there first){% else %}PR and CI state with `gh pr view {{ pr_number }} --json headRefOid,mergeable,mergeStateStatus`{% endif %} and `gh pr checks {{ pr_number }} --json name,state,bucket`. Never use `gh pr checks --watch` or parse its text output. Report a PR with no CI run as conflicting or needs-rebase, with the {% if sprint_scoped %}`gh stack view --json`{% else %}`gh pr view --json`{% endif %} output.</step>
    <step id="k">Install the daemon-readable templates with `mkdir -p ~/.atm/templates/quality-management-gh && cp .claude/skills/quality-management-gh/*.j2 ~/.atm/templates/quality-management-gh/`. Append the completed QA report to the PR whenever a PR exists: render `FAIL`/`IN-FLIGHT` with `atm compose --template ~/.atm/templates/quality-management-gh/findings-report.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/qa-<pr>-vars.json | gh pr comment <PR> --body-file -`, or `PASS` with the same command using `~/.atm/templates/quality-management-gh/quality-report.md.j2`. Build vars for this QA run from the selected template's `required_variables` frontmatter; never reuse a previous or sample report's vars. Write the vars file outside the repository working tree (in the session scratchpad or a temp directory); never commit or stage it, and delete it or let it expire after the send. The PR comment remains required.</step>
    <step id="l">Deliver the final QA verdict by closing this task with the rendered report as the body: `atm task close {{ task_id }} completed --template ~/.atm/templates/quality-management-gh/findings-report.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/qa-<pr>-vars.json` for `FAIL`/`IN-FLIGHT`, or `atm task close {{ task_id }} completed --template ~/.atm/templates/quality-management-gh/quality-report.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/qa-<pr>-vars.json` for `PASS`. The close delivers the report to the assigner (the sender of this message) and frees your queue; a plain `atm send {{ lead }}` leaves `{{ task_id }}` open and the next QA task never starts. A `FAIL` verdict still closes the task as `completed` — the QA round is the task, the code is not; use the same outside-repository scratchpad or temp vars file, never commit or stage it, and delete it or let it expire after the send. Include deliverable completion as `X/Y (Z%)`. PASS requires 100% deliverable completion, a PASS verdict from every required reviewer, an active PR for the branch, and no unresolved blocking findings. {% if cc and cc != lead %}Copy a one-line summary to `{{ cc }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc }} --stdin`.{% endif %}</step>
{% if triage_records and triage_records|trim %}    <step id="m">For every triage record in `{{ triage_records }}` (or any other `.triage/*/findings/*.ttl` record in scope for this round) that this round's reviewers confirm is fixed on the reviewed branch, update that record's `triage:status` to `"fixed"` and set `triage:closedAt`/`triage:closedBy "quality-mgr"` before sending the final verdict — per the triaging-findings skill's closure rule that quality-mgr, not {{ lead }}, owns finding closure once follow-up QA confirms the fix. Do not close a record that is still open, regressed, or unverified this round. List every record you closed (finding id + file) in the final verdict message so {{ lead }} can reconcile it.</step>
{% endif %}  </workflow>
</repo-qa-task>
{%- endautoescape %}
